Title: LAST LIGHT
Credit: An original demonstration screenplay
Author: Fount example contributors
Draft date: 2026-09-24

# Before the last ferry

INT. MARINA OFFICE - DUSK

MARA holds a soaked invoice against the light. A second set of numbers shows through. DAN levels a crooked picture instead of looking at it.

MARA
The boat on this invoice sank last winter.

DAN
We bill by the berth, not by altitude.

MARA
I need the original ledger before the office closes.

DAN
The original is with the accountant across the water.

[[Give Dan a response here that costs him something.]]

EXT. EQUIPMENT SHED - DUSK

Dan unlocks a tin cashbox. One brass key lies on a float shaped like a fish.

DAN
Take this. It opens the service gate on the ferry pier.

MARA
Why are you giving me your way out?

DAN
Because you keep mistaking a locked door for an answer.

He puts the key in her hand. She pockets it.

MARA
You can still come with me and explain the numbers.

DAN
Let me shut the place before you arrest the furniture.

/* Earlier draft: Dan hides the key under a cracked mug. */

INT. RECORDS ROOM - NIGHT

The carbon copy of the ledger lies open between them. Dan circles an overwritten total.

DAN
I forged the ledger. The money kept the repair crew here.

MARA
You used my signature to make it look approved.

DAN
I thought we could put it back before anybody noticed.

MARA
The accountant has the original. We take both copies to her.

Mara folds the carbon copy into her raincoat.

EXT. BOAT RAMP - NIGHT

Rain drums on a boat under repair. Mara holds a ladder while Dan straps down its tarp.

MARA
You forged my name and let me thank you for saving this place.

DAN
I did save it. I just cannot show you a clean receipt.

MARA
Then come to the ferry and say that where it counts.

He reaches the bottom rung. She lets go before his foot finds it. He catches himself.

[[Keep the imbalance between helping him and resenting him.]]

INT. TICKET HUT - NIGHT

The CLERK lowers the metal shutter. Mara pushes the damp invoice underneath.

MARA
Two seats. The accountant is expecting these tonight.

CLERK
The passenger ramp is closed. Last boat is loading freight.

DAN
We can use the service gate. I have a key.

Mara raises the fish-shaped float. Dan checks his empty pocket.

MARA
You used to have a key. I have a decision to make.

CLERK
Then make it walking. They leave when the truck clears.

EXT. FERRY QUEUE - NIGHT

A refrigerated truck inches toward the vessel. Mara counts the straps on its load as if one might stop it.

DAN
We could send the copies and stay here.

MARA
Paper does not answer questions. You are coming with me.

DAN
I can tell her where every penny went.

MARA
Start with why you needed my name on it.

He follows her past the truck.

EXT. SERVICE GATE - NIGHT

Mara fits the brass key into the lock. It sticks. She twists the fish float until the gate opens.

DAN
That key has never liked being hurried before.

MARA
Hold this open. We are finished asking permission.

Dan braces the gate while Mara crosses the wet pier. He could stay on this side. He steps through.

INT. FERRY SALOON - NIGHT

Empty plastic seats. Mara lays the two pages side by side. Dan starts smoothing a crease, then leaves it alone.

DAN
I will tell her what I did first.

MARA
Tell her while I am in the room.

The engines take the weight of the boat. Their untouched coffees tremble.

===
